 "answer": {
  "answer": "The study did not find any combination of ego speed and pedestrian timing that makes the policy fail, so it does not answer the question. It confirmed four of the five cases it was asked for, on four different scenes (S1, S2, S11, S29). All four are at the nominal setting (1.0x speed, 0 s shift), and none involves the pedestrian. The ego hit parked or moving vehicles or left the road after ignoring or misreading the route command. Not every failure is clearly the policy's fault: some S11 and S29 failures are \"unclear\" in the review, and every S1 failure is flagged as a possible simulator artifact. No run at any other speed or timing failed, but most of those settings ran only once, which is a hint, not a rate. Most of the speed\u2013timing grid was never run.",
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "Four scenes fail at the nominal setting (1.0x, 0 s) on every repeat, and the ranges put their failure rates above 0.5. These are the four confirmed cases. Because they fail without any speed or timing change, they fill top-k slots without saying anything about speed or timing.",
